WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.300
For years, React Native operated under a structural constraint.

2
00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:07.500
Every time JavaScript needed to command native hardware,

3
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.700
instructions crossed a separate intermediary layer.

4
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:13.400
Engineers call this the asynchronous bridge.

5
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.300
It acted as a single mandatory choke point.

6
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.600
If your app needed to access the camera,

7
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.200
read the battery level, or query a database,

8
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.200
the call had to travel across this specific bridge.

9
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.900
The new architecture of React Native removes this bridge

10
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:28.700
from the data path entirely.

11
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.100
This schematic shows the structural shift.

12
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.900
The asynchronous bridge is replaced by JSI,

13
00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.800
the JavaScript interface.

14
00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.700
JSI establishes a direct C++ channel

15
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.500
between the JavaScript environment and the native code,

16
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:44.900
creating an instantaneous two-way flow of information.

17
00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:47.400
By making this switch, React Native moves away

18
00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.600
from a serialized message passing system

19
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.900
and into a shared memory environment.

20
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.300
This enables three specific capabilities

21
00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.000
that were previously impossible.

22
00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.600
Synchronous execution, absolute type safety

23
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.000
at the boundary, and lazy initialization.

24
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.000
Turbo modules utilize these mechanics

25
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.500
to completely rewrite how JavaScript commands

26
00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:08.500
the device's native mobile hardware from the ground up.

27
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.100
To isolate exactly why the asynchronous bridge

28
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.900
caused performance lag, we can trace the execution

29
00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:17.300
of a single native module call under the legacy architecture.

30
00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.500
In the legacy bridge pipeline, a data object

31
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.100
cannot cross directly.

32
00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:22.840
It's crushed into a JSON string,

33
00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:24.900
queued, and pushed across the gap.

34
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:27.400
Every argument underwent this serialization.

35
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.700
On the native side, it unpacks into a generic readable map.

36
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:33.700
This unpacking process exposes the second failure point,

37
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:35.800
a lack of type safety at the boundary.

38
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:38.500
Because the incoming data arrived as a generic map,

39
00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:40.500
the native code had no strict guarantee

40
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:41.700
of what it was receiving.

41
00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.400
It had to guess the contents.

42
00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:46.500
The native side would mechanically fish out values

43
00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.700
by looking up specific string keys at runtime,

44
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:52.600
hoping the correct data type was attached to them.

45
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:54.500
If the JavaScript sent a number,

46
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:56.800
but the native code expected a string,

47
00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.700
the system wouldn't catch the error during the build.

48
00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.800
The app would simply crash in the user's hands.

49
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:06.000
Because the entire queue operated asynchronously,

50
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.100
the JavaScript thread could not pause

51
00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:10.700
and wait to see if the operation succeeded.

52
00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:14.000
It fired the message and immediately moved on.

53
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.800
The legacy architecture traded processing speed

54
00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.900
and type safety for a simpler connection model,

55
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.500
imposing a hard ceiling on the complexity and responsiveness

56
00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:25.800
of react native applications.

57
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:27.400
Under the new architecture,

58
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:30.200
that ceiling is removed by integrating directly

59
00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:32.500
with the Hermes JavaScript engine.

60
00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:35.000
This diagram shows the new stack.

61
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.200
JSI is a C++ abstraction layer,

62
00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.800
locking directly onto the Hermes engine.

63
00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.800
Within this layer sits the core primitive, the host object.

64
00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:47.400
Hermes interacts with this block directly,

65
00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.500
treating it exactly like a native JavaScript object.

66
00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:53.800
To the JavaScript thread, a host object looks and behaves

67
00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:56.100
exactly like a normal JS object.

68
00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:00.000
But mechanically, its methods are actual C++ lambdas.

69
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.900
This relationship is the basis for the new system.

70
00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:06.000
Every turbo module is implemented as a host object.

71
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.200
We can see exactly how this bypasses the old bottleneck

72
00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.700
by tracing a practical code execution.

73
00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:16.000
When the JavaScript thread fires nativecalculator.add1.2,

74
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:19.200
the input traces directly into the C++ execution block

75
00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:20.500
with 0Q.

76
00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:22.800
Because nativecalculator is a host object,

77
00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:25.000
no JSON serialization occurs.

78
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:27.400
C++ instantly calculates the result

79
00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:30.400
and hands it back along the exact same shared memory line.

80
00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:33.200
The JSI runtime calls the C++ lambda directly

81
00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:35.000
on the JavaScript thread itself.

82
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:38.500
By eliminating the ASync queue and operating in shared memory,

83
00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:41.400
turbo modules can finally expose synchronous native methods

84
00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:42.500
to JavaScript.

85
00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:45.400
While JSI solves the speed problem,

86
00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:48.200
the architecture still needs a way to guarantee

87
00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:52.200
the native side knows exactly what data JavaScript is sending.

88
00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:55.000
To solve this, every turbo module requires

89
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.500
a strict TypeScript specification file

90
00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:00.700
defining the exact inputs and outputs.

91
00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:04.000
That specification document is processed by Cogen,

92
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:06.500
splitting into strict typed interfaces

93
00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:10.400
for C++, Java, and Objective C++.

94
00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:13.500
Cogen reads the TypeScript spec at build time,

95
00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.600
generating typed native interfaces

96
00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:17.600
before the app compiles completely

97
00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:21.700
eliminates blindly extracting values from maps at runtime.

98
00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:24.300
Beyond execution speed and type safety,

99
00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:28.100
turbo modules also alter application startup performance.

100
00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:30.400
The legacy bridge eagerly instantiated

101
00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.700
every single native module the moment the app opened,

102
00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:36.300
burning CPU cycles and memory on features

103
00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:38.400
the user hadn't even touched yet.

104
00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:40.700
This chart tracks CPU and memory load

105
00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:42.600
during application startup.

106
00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:44.900
Legacy spikes heavily at zero seconds

107
00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:46.400
as all modules load.

108
00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:48.800
Turbo modules maintain a flat line,

109
00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:52.400
utilizing a factory pattern for lazy initialization.

110
00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:56.200
By default, modules are set to avoid eager initialization.

111
00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:59.000
Memory is only consumed the exact millisecond

112
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.100
the JavaScript thread actively requests

113
00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:03.100
the module from the registry.

114
00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:05.500
By combining Cogen's build time safety

115
00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:07.800
with lazy runtime initialization,

116
00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:10.500
turbo modules create a system that is type safe

117
00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:13.800
and uses fewer device resources during startup.

118
00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.300
Moving an ecosystem to a new architecture

119
00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:19.000
requires managing existing code bases,

120
00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:21.100
particularly apps that rely on older,

121
00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:23.600
un-migrated third-party modules.

122
00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:26.200
This comparison matrix breaks down module behavior

123
00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:28.000
across architectures.

124
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.000
To prevent legacy code from breaking,

125
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.600
react native 0.76 and above

126
00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:36.000
includes an automatic turbo module interop layer.

127
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:38.400
The interop layer wraps old native modules

128
00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.120
in a turbo module facade,

129
00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:42.600
functioning without immediate rewrites.

130
00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:45.400
However, this facade only provides compatibility,

131
00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:46.800
not performance.

132
00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:50.000
A wrapped module still lacks lazy initialization

133
00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:51.300
and type safety.

134
00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:53.900
To realize the full architectural benefits,

135
00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:56.900
a module must satisfy four definitive properties

136
00:05:56.900 --> 00:06:00.100
to be classified as a true native turbo module.

137
00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:02.200
It must possess a TypeScript spec.

138
00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:04.500
It must be compiled using Cogen.

139
00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:07.300
It must communicate directly via JSI.

140
00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:09.400
It must be lazy by default.

141
00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:12.700
Satisfying these properties removes the async bottleneck.

142
00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:14.600
It completes react native's evolution

143
00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:16.200
from a bridge-dependent framework

144
00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:19.200
into a fully unified memory-shared environment.

